data.content:
No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since you said "class library with no DI set up," the lightest option depends on your .NET version:

### .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

Inject it via the constructor — no DI container required:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Production:** `new InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider.System)`

**Tests** (add NuGet `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(fake.GetUtcNow().AddDays(-1)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(fake.GetUtcNow().AddDays(1)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(2));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(fake.GetUtcNow().AddDays(-1))); // now overdue
```

### Pre-.NET 8 → Ambient `Clock` helper (no constructor changes needed)

If adding constructor parameters is too disruptive, use an `AsyncLocal`-based ambient context:

```csharp
public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

Then replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` → `Clock.UtcNow`, and in tests:

```csharp
using (Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)))
{
    Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(Clock.UtcNow.AddDays(-1)));
}
```

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — it's the framework-blessed solution, zero custom code, and `FakeTimeProvider` gives you full control. Want me to apply the change to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
